WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.440
Did Spider-Man make an unapproved cameo in the&nbsp;
X-Men animated series? Ever wonder how popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.640
the show was when it first premiered? This is&nbsp;
the untold truth of "X-Men: The Animated Series."

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.280
In the 1980s, Margaret Loesch was&nbsp;
working as a producer in TV animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.120
helping to get shows like G.I. Joe and&nbsp;
Muppet Babies on the air. But she still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.640
couldn't get her passion project off the ground:&nbsp;
a cartoon adaptation of Marvel's X-Men comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.920
While working at Hanna-Barbera, Loesch met Stan&nbsp;
Lee, Marvel's prolific creator and company face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.200
and pitched him on an X-Men show. She recalled&nbsp;
telling Lee in the book Previously on X-Men,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.160
"It's really about a group&nbsp;
of disenfranchised teenagers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.920
and a lot of teenagers feel that way …&nbsp;
so why wouldn't this be a hit with kids?"

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.520
Lee was convinced, and the two then set&nbsp;
out to pitch the X-Men show. Alas, ABC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:54.560
NBC, and CBS all passed. Loesch had all but given&nbsp;
up until a fourth broadcast network emerged. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.200
eventually became head of Fox Kids, the division&nbsp;
of the Fox network responsible for its Saturday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.880
morning lineup. So in her new position,&nbsp;
she commissioned an X-Men series in 1992.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.080
The creative staff for X-Men: The Animated&nbsp;
Series came together in early 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.640
with Fox looking to premiere the series in the&nbsp;
fall of that year. Considering the long production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.440
lead times required for animation, showrunner&nbsp;
Eric Lewald's team had mere weeks to come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.720
with a "series bible," which is a 20-to-100 page&nbsp;
document outlining the show's world, characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.680
and aims. Lewald basically had to figure&nbsp;
out the entire series in that short period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.680
and it didn't help that he wasn't intimately&nbsp;
familiar with the X-Men comic books.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.360
A first step toward acquainting himself and his&nbsp;
team with the mutant superheroes would be to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.440
acquire as many X-Men back issues as possible, but&nbsp;
that proved difficult. The title was relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.960
obscure, and Lewald had to make do with whatever&nbsp;
source material he could find. He procured it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.600
in a haphazard way, buying up old anthologies,&nbsp;
borrowing issues from friends, and getting Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.360
Comics to black-and-white photocopy old issues and&nbsp;
mail them. Most of the show bible was even heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.320
influenced by the X-Men board game The Uncanny&nbsp;
X-Men Special! Campaign Set, which included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.440
character descriptions, a map of the X-Mansion,&nbsp;
and The Official Handbook of the Marvel Universe.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.680
While the look and feel of X-Men:&nbsp;
The Animated Series comes from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.440
post-1975 reboot of the comic series, Eric&nbsp;
Lewald didn't directly adapt old issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.440
That's in part because a comic book's&nbsp;
method of storytelling is significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.960
different from that of a TV show. As Lewald&nbsp;
explained to Blast from the Past in 2007,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.400
"Time the actual action in a book,&nbsp;
and you get about three minutes."

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.400
Longer arcs from the comics were a better&nbsp;
fit for adaptation, but as Lewald noted,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.040
"There we had to deal with the various secondary&nbsp;
plots that weave in and out of the main story."

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.440
Some of those plots worked for a children's&nbsp;
cartoon, while others didn't. As Lewald put it,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.280
"If you were to compare the actual '[Dark]&nbsp;
Phoenix' books to the animation story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.400
I'd wager that about 50 percent&nbsp;
of the original was trimmed away."

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.760
Furthermore, Lewald's team couldn't&nbsp;
just take old material and animate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.480
it because the public at large&nbsp;
wasn't terribly familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.880
the vagaries of the franchise. As&nbsp;
Sewald told Comics Beat in 2017,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.280
"When we started, we were&nbsp;
telling stories primarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.760
for people unfamiliar with the X-Men world."

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.080
"No one knows who will be a mutant at birth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.809
We discover our extraordinary&nbsp;
powers at about your age."

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.480
X-Men: The Animated Series features a massive&nbsp;
cast of characters. In addition to those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.800
enjoyed regular appearances, many more&nbsp;
mutants arrived for cameos, one-offs, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.920
to populate finite story arcs and then disappear&nbsp;
thereafter. Deciding what mutants from the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.120
to use as the main characters was especially&nbsp;
challenging for the cartoon's developers. Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.120
helped the show's writers determine which of&nbsp;
them were must-haves, such as Professor X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.240
Cyclops, Wolverine, and Jean Grey. But,&nbsp;
as Eric Lewald told Blast from the Past,

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.600
"Beyond that, any out of a&nbsp;
couple dozen major mutants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.280
could have filled out the rest of the core."

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.840
Lewald and his team decided on Storm because her&nbsp;
ability to control the weather would translate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.360
well in a visual medium like TV. Rogue was&nbsp;
picked because her inability to be touched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.760
offered an emotionally compelling characteristic,&nbsp;
plus her strength and flight would work well in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.320
fight sequences. Additionally, Lewald knew&nbsp;
that the show needed a juvenile character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.640
and Marvel reps liked Jubilee more than&nbsp;
other young mutants. And then Morph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.360
based on a relatively obscure comic character&nbsp;
named Changeling, was added for the specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:24.000
purpose of having a sympathetic friend of&nbsp;
Wolverine who would be killed early on.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.709
"This one's for you, Morph!"

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.720
Fox ordered X-Men in February 1992 and wanted to&nbsp;
debut it that fall — a rather daunting prospect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.760
But somehow, Eric Lewald and company were ready&nbsp;
for their premiere date, and X-Men: The Animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:46.000
Series debuted on Fox on October 31, 1992, with&nbsp;
the episode "Night of the Sentinels: Part 1."

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.880
Fox was so confident in the series and wanted to&nbsp;
give it as big of a launch as possible that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.080
aired the initial episode at 7:00 PM. The series&nbsp;
then moved to its regular Saturday morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.720
time slot, albeit sporadically. "Night of&nbsp;
the Sentinels, Part 2" aired on November 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.360
but then the quickly building audience was&nbsp;
left without a new episode for three weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.280
And then the next new installments&nbsp;
didn't air until late January.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.040
According to Lewald, the gap was because&nbsp;
of animation problems, which took months to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.480
fix. This led to major production delays. But&nbsp;
once the show got going with regular airings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.800
ratings soared and X-Men became one of the&nbsp;
biggest hits in Saturday morning history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.520
As many as 8 million homes&nbsp;
tuned in, and some weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.240
it would attract more viewers than the&nbsp;
competition on ABC, CBS, and NBC combined.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.080
X-Men: The Animated Series marks a watershed&nbsp;
moment in both comic adaptations and Saturday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.880
morning cartoons. It was one of the first shows to&nbsp;
present Marvel Comics characters and stories in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.760
serious and dramatic way, and it was also one&nbsp;
of the few kids cartoons that was serialized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.240
Making such an ambitious series would&nbsp;
require a very special cast of voice actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.560
and X-Men producers found them, though not&nbsp;
right away. The casting and voice directors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.120
who were used to wacky Saturday morning cartoons,&nbsp;
didn't understand the show's vibe, so they hired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.880
actors who did a lot of silly, broad voices. As&nbsp;
Eric Lewald told The Hollywood Reporter in 2017,

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.760
"We tried to convey to them&nbsp;
what was different about X-Men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.600
and they didn't hear it. They thought, 'They&nbsp;
want to do something goofy and childish.'"

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.080
The initial recordings were so bad that the&nbsp;
crew had to throw everything out and start over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.880
The voice team worked out of&nbsp;
Canada and recruited veterans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.560
of the Toronto entertainment world,&nbsp;
including Alyson Court as Jubilee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.960
and Cal Dodd as Wolverine. Interestingly&nbsp;
enough, those two already knew each other.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.520
"Is the child all right?"
"Not for long."&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.240
"They were fighting, and I&nbsp;
wanted to help him, and boom!"

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.320
For many Marvel fans, Cal Dodd's&nbsp;
portrayal of Wolverine on X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.720
The Animated Series is the definitive take&nbsp;
on the clawed and conflicted Canadian mutant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.600
Dodd pulled from a number of disparate sources&nbsp;
to get the voice just right. During an audition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:52.640
a casting director told him to emulate tough-guy&nbsp;
actor Steve McQueen because Wolverine is similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.960
introspective and quiet. As Dodd recalled&nbsp;
to The Hollywood Reporter, he also threw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.160
Clint Eastwood and Western star Ward Bond into&nbsp;
the mix. Meanwhile, Wolverine's scrappiness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.960
came straight from Dodd, who is a Canadian as&nbsp;
well. As he revealed to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.720
"I had grown up in a small town with many&nbsp;
fights. I had seen these kinds of guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.240
so I threw in a bit of [raspy&nbsp;
voiced DJ] Wolfman Jack."

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.080
Dodd is proud of his work and how it affected&nbsp;
the viewers, including one in particular.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.560
An overweight, bullied, and depressed fan&nbsp;
sent a letter in which he revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.600
he had considered suicide. But, as Dodd recalled,

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.680
"He was so in love with the X-Men Saturday&nbsp;
morning cartoon and Wolverine [...] He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.280
about to jump off his roof, but then&nbsp;
he said 'I can't do this, I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.360
miss the Saturday morning X-Men episode.'&nbsp;
That's the kind of impact it had on kids."

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.480
"You called me friend."

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.600
One of the most memorable elements of X-Men:&nbsp;
The Animated Series is its opening sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.560
While character-identifying clips and scenes&nbsp;
of mutant battles fly by at frenetic pace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.840
the soundtrack offers a suitably high-energy theme&nbsp;
song built around a speedy, ominous, and totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:58.800
catchy hard rock guitar riff. And let’s not&nbsp;
forget those attention-grabbing keyboard stabs.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.680
The theme song has brought a lot of joy to a lot&nbsp;
of people, even independently of the series, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:09.280
amateur cover versions are all over YouTube. But&nbsp;
it wasn't such a lovely experience actually making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.720
the song, according to composer Ron Wasserman.&nbsp;
He wrote several songs for X-Men producer Saban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.480
Entertainment in the '90s, including the&nbsp;
theme for Mighty Morphin' Power Rangers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.360
Wasserman claims that the company cut him off from&nbsp;
talking to anyone at Marvel or Fox about how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:28.320
song should sound, fearing that they'd poach him&nbsp;
for higher-profile composing work. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:33.840
representatives of Saban were very hands-on and&nbsp;
critical. As Wasserman told Inverse in 2016,

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.320
"[X-Men] was just a boatload of work&nbsp;
because there was a lot of involvement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.560
A lot of, 'Let's tweak this, make this&nbsp;
bigger, redo this.' We ended up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.960
80 or 90 tracks for that thing. They just kept&nbsp;
trying to get more and more energy out of it."

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.320
Superheroes popping up in movies and TV shows&nbsp;
about other characters is an almost mundane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.120
phenomenon in the 21st century. For example,&nbsp;
Thor: Ragnarok heavily involved the Hulk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:02.000
Spider-Man was a major part of Captain America:&nbsp;
Civil War, and dozens of comic book legends all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.360
converged together in Avengers: Endgame. But back&nbsp;
in the early 90s, shared movie and/or TV universes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.520
weren't a matter of course, especially since the&nbsp;
rights to different characters created by the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.760
publishing entity had been sold to several&nbsp;
different studios and production companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.000
Nevertheless, X-Men: The Animated Series&nbsp;
dabbled in crossovers and universe sharing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.040
even though doing so could&nbsp;
violate copyrights and contracts.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.920
As show artist and self-proclaimed treasure hider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.280
Larry Houston revealed to The&nbsp;
Hollywood Reporter in 2017,

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:32.960
"Some of my favorite Easter eggs were the&nbsp;
unexpected ones that I added for the fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.960
like Doctor Strange, Deadpool, the Black&nbsp;
Panther, all of whom are now superstars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.360
in their own feature films. I never added&nbsp;
cameos if it distracted from the main story."

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.160
Higher-ups heard that Houston&nbsp;
had added Spider-Man to a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.320
and ordered it deleted. So, as he recalled,

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.520
"I had to sneak him into another&nbsp;
episode, but it was just an arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.560
shooting webbing to save someone&nbsp;
off-camera from falling debris."

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:58.080
Obviously, X-Men: The Animated Series is based&nbsp;
on Marvel's various and long-running X-Men comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.720
book series. But the show proved so popular&nbsp;
that it ended up also being influential in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.040
the development of the franchise in the other&nbsp;
direction. Over time, things introduced by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:11.760
show's writers were integrated into the comics,&nbsp;
making them canonical to the X-Men universe.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:18.320
The 1995 to 1996 X-Men comics crossover event "Age&nbsp;
of Apocalypse" was directly inspired by the X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:23.360
The Animated Series two-part episode "One Man's&nbsp;
Worth." The premise focused on a time-hopping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:27.440
alternate history adventure in which&nbsp;
characters go back in time to kill Professor X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.480
thereby altering the present and sending several&nbsp;
X-Men into the future. Eric Sewald conceived the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:38.640
idea, but it came to him from media outside the&nbsp;
X-Men universe. As he told Comics Beat in 2017,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.080
"Most good stories have been told&nbsp;
many times in many different forms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.720
I 'borrowed' from It's a Wonderful Life and [the&nbsp;
Star Trek episode] 'City on the Edge of Forever'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.640
for the central idea: 'What would the world&nbsp;
have been like without this one crucial man?'"

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.440
"It's incredible how one kid could&nbsp;
mean so much to so many people."

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.480
